{:edb/db-before-t 2009 :edb/db-after-t 2010 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535322 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:02:35.602Z" 13194139535322 true] [17592186192300 1100 "Compute $P\\left( X > 8 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062347/assets/cc8c2867f9ef99a8c476feeb319e03a3a479dd23f04e546bf1e5240daa4ea0ac.png)" 13194139535322 true] [17592186192300 1100 "Compute $P\\left(X > 8 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n${5e^{-5x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139535322 false] [17592186192300 1145 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$, then\n\n$$\nP(a < X < b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 8) &= P(8 < X < ∞) \\\\\n&= ∫_{8}^{∞}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{8}^{∞}5e^{-5x}dx \\\\\n&= [- e^{-5x}]_{8}^{∞} \\\\\n&= e^{-40}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535322 false] [17592186192300 1145 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a <    X <    b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(8<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-5x}]}_{8}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535322 true] [17592186192301 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062347/assets/0ba485cc9b536bd1a7cd18f37c61d269515b2bcb621cabfa55d6a567d53144c6.png)" 13194139535322 true] [17592186192301 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n${(k)/((4x + 1)^{3}),\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139535322 false] [17592186192301 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3}$ on the interval $x \\geq 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3} \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(4x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{[\\frac{k}{8(4x+1)^{2}}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{8} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = 8.\n$$" 13194139535322 true] [17592186192301 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nIn our case, for the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3}\n$$\n\non the interval\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nthe second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3} \\, \\textrm dx = 1\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{∞}(k)/((4x + 1)^{3})dx &= - [(k)/(8(4x + 1)^{2})]_{0}^{∞} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{8} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = 8\n$$" 13194139535322 false] [17592186192355 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062347/assets/d6ad9cbcf369609541984bb88a414653161a9ed723ec2637d6e73cc81b62a238.png)\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\geq 3$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{\\infty} f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139535322 true] [17592186192355 1100 "${(1)/((x - 2)^{2}),\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 3, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\geq 3$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{\\infty} f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139535322 false] [17592186192355 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x \\geq 3$, we have $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^2} \\geq 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$∫_{3}^{∞}f(x)dx|= ∫_{3}^{∞}(1)/((x - 2)^{2})dx; = ∫_{3}^{∞}(x - 2)^{-2}dx; =-\\frac{1}{x - 2} \\mid _{3}^{∞}; = - (0 - 1); = 1$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535322 false] [17592186192355 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x \\geq 3,$ we have $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^2} \\geq 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }(x-2)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,{|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535322 true] [17592186192579 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343630 13194139535322 true] [17592186192579 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343637 13194139535322 true] [17592186192579 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343644 13194139535322 true] [17592186192579 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343700 13194139535322 true] [17592186192580 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343679 13194139535322 true] [17592186192580 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343686 13194139535322 true] [17592186192580 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343693 13194139535322 true] [17592186192580 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343707 13194139535322 true] [17592186192626 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343651 13194139535322 true] [17592186192626 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343658 13194139535322 true] [17592186192626 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343665 13194139535322 true] [17592186192626 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343672 13194139535322 true] [17592186192966 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535322 true] [17592186192966 1100 "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139535322 true] [17592186192966 1100 "$f(x) = {e^{1 - x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\ge 0$ for all $x \\ge 1$\n2. $∫_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139535322 false] [17592186192966 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1$, we have $f(x)={e}^{1-x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{1-x}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535322 true] [17592186193217 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535322 true] [17592186193217 1100 "Compute $P(X \\ge 5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$f(x) = {8e^{-8x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x < 0$." 13194139535322 false] [17592186193217 1100 "Compute $P(X\\ge 5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-8x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535322 true] [17592186193217 1145 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(5\\le X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-8x}]}_{5}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535322 true] [17592186193218 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535322 true] [17592186193218 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535322 true] [17592186193218 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$f(x) = {ke^{-3x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139535322 false] [17592186193218 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-3x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$" 13194139535322 true] [17592186343630 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343631 13194139535322 true] [17592186343630 1089 "q-126016" 13194139535322 true] [17592186343630 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535322 true] [17592186343630 1100 "Compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535322 true] [17592186343630 1113 #uuid "87ab5e1a-40f2-585e-8f36-c410606655e8" 13194139535322 true] [17592186343630 1145 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =P(3\\le X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x}]}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535322 true] [17592186343631 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343632 13194139535322 true] [17592186343631 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343633 13194139535322 true